# act reads this before it runs anything, and its only job is to stop act
# asking which runner image to use. With no config anywhere, act opens an
# interactive menu; a redirected or piped run answers that menu with EOF and
# then starts a container with an empty image name, which surfaces as
# `invalid reference format` and looks like a Docker problem rather than a
# missing answer to a question nobody saw.
#
# catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest is the medium image act itself recommends.
# It is not GitHub's runner image and does not pretend to be: it has no
# rustup, which is why ci.yml installs rustup when the runner has none.
# Both runner labels, and both pointed at the same Linux image. act skips a job
# whose platform it has no mapping for, and a matrix job is skipped whole: with
# only ubuntu-latest here, `act -W ci.yml` quietly ran the two jobs that are not
# a matrix and silently dropped clippy and the tests, which is the one job worth
# running. It says so, in a line that scrolls past among a hundred others, and
# then the run ends green.
#
# The macOS leg on this image is not macOS and does not pretend to be. What it
# checks is that the workflow's steps are right, which is all act ever checks;
# the platform difference is what the real runner is for.
-P ubuntu-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest
-P macos-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest
